    "q-142884": {
      "dom_id": "step-q142884",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ |\n\nCompute $P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=3)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above.",
      "worked_solution": "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(Y=0\\,|\\,X=3)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=0)}{P(X=3)}=\\frac{f(3,0)}{{f}_{X}(3)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(3) & =f(3,0)+f(3,1)+f(3,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{18}+\\frac{2}{18}+\\frac{1}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(3,0)=\\frac{1}{9}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting ${f}_{X}(3)=\\frac{5}{18}$ and $f(3,0)=\\frac{1}{9}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=3) & =\\frac{f(3,0)}{{f}_{X}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{9})}{(\\frac{5}{18})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
